Tier convention: registry stores single-letter tier codes
("V" = V1, "U" = U1, "P" = P, "ML" = ML). CoverageOK accepts any
non-empty Tier string; for this slice Tier="V" matches the
predecessor V1 entries (e.g. range-prediction-model).

Empty RouteSet fields MUST be explicit `[]string{}`. Nil slices
fail CoverageOK and aivet's route audit.


=== REASONING ===

Why this design preserves ADR-015 + P1-P10:

I3 baseline intact — voice mode is layered ABOVE the existing
chatbot conversation grid; the typed Textarea + send button and
useChatSessions / useChatHistory plumbing are untouched. A user
with ai_mode='off' sees exactly the same ChatbotPage they saw
before, minus the AIChatbotIndicator and AIVoiceMode panels
(both gated via withAiFeature → return null). The two AI
surfaces are independently gateable so a deployment can ship
chatbot-LLM without voice-mode (or vice versa).

I4 zero egress — STT and TTS are pure browser APIs
(window.SpeechRecognition / window.speechSynthesis). The ONLY
network bytes leaving the browser are the transcribed text +
session_id over POST /api/v1/ai/voice/chat — the same shape as
the typed chatbot's POST. Raw audio bytes never cross the
network. The handler routes the call through the same dispatcher +
decorator chain as the chatbot LLM (redaction → rate-limit →
cost-cap → audit → trace), inheriting all chatbot-LLM safeguards.

I5 hidden UI — AIVoiceMode is wrapped in
withAiFeature('voice-mode', InnerSection). In off mode the HOC
returns null; the DOM contains nothing for this feature. The
load-bearing off-mode test asserts the data-testid
'ai-feature-voice-mode-root' is absent, that no role-button
matching /Speak to Helix/i exists, that no mic/tts/transcript
sub-test-IDs exist, and that the localStorage key
'ai.voiceMode.transcriptDraft' is never written. A positive-
control case proves the gate actually works when toggled on.

I6 404 routes — POST /api/v1/ai/voice/chat is mounted under
`g.Wrap("voice-mode", h.HandleStream)` in ai_routes.go. The
Go-side TestVoiceModeAIOffNoVoiceControlsOrStorage asserts the
route returns 404 in off mode AND that the baseline chatbot
POST /api/v1/ai/chatbot/chat is unaffected (still gated on
'chatbot-llm', not 'voice-mode').

I7 per-feature — `voice-mode` ships with DefaultOn=false; the
HOC requires the per-feature flag to be explicitly true even
when ai_mode is non-off. The off-mode test exercises both
halves of the gate (mode='off' + toggle=true, then mode='cloud'
+ toggle=false) and the positive control (mode='cloud' +
toggle=true).

I10 type system — the request body is a closed Go struct
`aiVoiceModeRequest{Message, SessionID}` with json tags; bad
shapes return HTTP 400. The tool envelope `VoiceModeEnvelope`
is a closed shape with pre-computed display-unit fields
(VehicleSnapshot omits GPS/street names by construction; battery
percent is range-clamped int via coerceVoiceModeIntPercent so a
runaway sensor reading can't leak as "battery is at 250
percent"). The strategy's allowedTools whitelist (literal
string slice) prevents the LLM from calling any other tool.

I12 client/bg — service-worker chunk `ai-voice-mode` and
storage key `ai.voiceMode.transcriptDraft` are declared in
the registry and bounded to the AIVoiceMode mount lifecycle.
A dedicated useEffect clears the draft on unmount; the off-
mode test proves the key is never written when the component
is not mounted.

P1 provider port — handler resolves provider via
providerFactory; tool code never touches provider directly.

P3 strategy in own package — internal/ai/strategies/voice-mode/
contains strategy.go + strategy_test.go + goldens.yaml +
canned/*.yaml. The strategy declares System prompt, Policy=
PolicyChatbot (Allow=nil, ModeRedactedTags), and an
allowedTools whitelist of just `stream_chatbot_response`.

P4 dispatcher loop — handler calls dispatch.Run(ctx, ...)
with the constructed strategy + tools + redactionStrip + writer.
The denyAllConfirm callback rejects every F4 confirm request:
voice mode is read-only and does not propose mutations.

P5 decorator chain — provider is wrapped by the standard
redaction → rate-limit → cost-cap → audit → trace chain via
providerFactory. The voice handler does NOT bypass any
decorator.

P6 guard — POST /api/v1/ai/voice/chat is wrapped with
`g.Wrap("voice-mode", h.HandleStream)`. The frontend gates via
useAiEnabled('voice-mode') + withAiFeature('voice-mode',
InnerSection). Both halves of the contract are exercised by
the load-bearing tests.

P8 goldens — 3 deterministic goldens cover: ask_battery_status,
ask_for_a_setting_change (read-only refusal), and
ambiguous_request_clarifies (asks for clarification). Each
golden's TTS-hostile syntax (no %, no markdown bullets, no
URLs, no "I have just" claims) is pinned by the strategy_test.

P9 registry — voice-mode entry added with Tier="V",
DefaultOn=false, NeedsRAG=false, NeedsTools=true, NeedsStream=true,
explicit Routes{Backend, Frontend, UITestIDs, JobNames=[]string{},
PushKinds=[]string{}}. aivet and aigen --check both pass.

P10 baseline coexistence — VoiceModeController is selected by
ai_mode + voice-mode toggle. The text Textarea + send remain
the canonical baseline; voice mode is purely additive.

P11/P12 (W1 inline wiring) — AIVoiceMode imports useAiStream,
posts to /ai/voice/chat (the registered backend path),
disables the action button via the COMPUTED expression
`!canStart || isBusy`, and emits zero placeholder strings.
The on-mode wiring test proves the button click POSTs exactly
once to /api/v1/ai/voice/chat with the dictated transcript +
session_id in the body, and that a second click while
streaming is a no-op (double-submit guard).

HX scaffold — AIVoiceMode renders through AIFeatureCard with
the per-feature verb passed as `buttonLabel="Speak to Helix"`
(NOT "Ask Helix" — the card composes the accessible name as
"Ask Helix · Speak to Helix"). Assistant-identity glyphs come
from HelixMark via AIFeatureCard's AIBadge + button icon.
i18n keys under voiceMode.* say "Helix" not "AI".

Why ai/ar/he get only en.json updates — the established Phase-46
placeholder convention (web/src/i18n/{ar,he}.json _meta note)
defers translation to a follow-up sweep; all missing keys fall
back to en.json via i18next's fallbackLng. The predecessor 0054
slice did the same. Adding voice-mode keys to ar.json/he.json
in English would be a single isolated entry in otherwise
empty placeholder files; following precedent keeps the locale
ports consistent for the eventual translation sweep.

R4 SI canonicalization — the voice-mode tool envelope emits
pre-computed display-unit fields (miles for distance, percent
for battery) rather than letting the LLM do unit arithmetic on
SI meters. This mirrors the cToFPtr precedent in
drive_coaching.go and avoids the "LLM does math on negative
fractions" failure mode.

R2 (Phase-48 audit-pin equivalent) — coerceVoiceModeIntPercent
range-clamps [0,100] so a wire spike of e.g. 250 returns
(0, false) and the snapshot field is omitted entirely rather
than reaching the LLM as "battery is at 250 percent".


